# Deliberately NOT `* text=auto`.
#
# That would renormalise every file in the repo in one commit, and this checkout
# is shared with another agent mid-work. The line-ending warnings on every commit
# are cosmetic and stay.
#
# What is here is the case that is not cosmetic: files whose bytes are the point.
# assets/posecode-embed-0.1.0.js carries its own sha256 in a comment on
# player-animations.html, and git's text detection would hand a Windows clone a
# CRLF copy that no longer matches the hash the page publishes. The tensors and
# the font are binary in fact; saying so keeps any future heuristic from deciding
# otherwise.

assets/posecode-embed-0.1.0.js         -text
public/assets/posecode-embed-0.1.0.js  -text

*.f32    -text
*.bin    -text
*.woff2  -text
*.png    -text
*.ico    -text

# Every file under assets/ whose content hash becomes a `?v=` cache token has to
# have the same bytes everywhere, or the token a Windows checkout stamps will not
# describe the bytes Vercel serves. Measured on this box, with core.autocrlf
# true: `git checkout -- assets/map-camera.js` turned 16,869 bytes into 17,231
# with 362 CRLF, and check_frontend immediately reported the tokens on three
# pages as stale. Same mechanism, wider blast radius than the one bundle.

# These four rules were right about the mechanism and too narrow about the paths.
# `assets/*.js` does not match `assets/lemmino/drift-void.js`, `assets/js/*.js`,
# `assets/posecode/parser.js` or `assets/workers/*.js`, and nothing covered .json
# at all — so assets/data/*.json and every nested bundle still came out of a
# Windows checkout with CRLF. Measured: 56 files under assets/ differ that way,
# and stamp_assets.py on this box therefore wrote tokens describing bytes that
# only exist here. The same --check passed locally and failed on ubuntu with 12
# pages stale, which is the drift arriving exactly where the comment predicted.
#
# `**` matches at any depth, so these subsume the four narrow rules.

assets/**            -text
public/assets/**     -text
